  "narrative": "The enrichment analysis is complete and all membership and path queries are verified. Here is the final narrative:\n\n---\n\nThe differential metabolome is strongly anchored to **galactose metabolism** (KEGG map00052; FDR = 0, fold-enrichment 950.9) and its disease counterpart **galactosemia** (SMP00182; FDR = 0, fold-enrichment 982.6). Six of the eleven metabolites—galactitol (C01697), myo-inositol (C00137), galactinol (C01235), D-glucose (C00221), sucrose (C00089), and glycerol (C00116)—map to this pathway with perfect co-occurrence plausibility (1.0). The enrichment is driven by the sugar alcohols and glycosyl donors: galactitol is the characteristic downstream metabolite of galactose flux, galactinol serves as the galactosyl donor for raffinose-family oligosaccharide synthesis, and sucrose provides the fructose moiety that can be redirected into galactose-related pools. Glycerol appears as a terminal catabolite of galactose degradation.\n\nWithin the galactose network, **D-glucose (C00221) is upstream of galactitol (C01697)** via the Leloir pathway and downstream sugar alcohols (path length = 5 via C00267 → C00794 → C05399 → C00124 → C01697; bidirectional resolution). This directionality is consistent with a block in downstream galactose catabolism that causes galactitol to accumulate—a hallmark of classical galactosemia. The secondary enriched pathway, **glycerophospholipid biosynthesis** (WP2533; FDR = 7.9 × 10⁻⁵), shares three metabolites (myo-inositol, ethanolamine, glycerol) and may reflect altered membrane lipid turnover downstream of metabolic stress.\n\nThe remaining metabolites—dopamine, 2-methoxyestradiol, inosine 2',3'-cyclic phosphate, and squalene—do not reach statistical pathway threshold but may indicate parallel perturbation in neurotransmitter metabolism, steroid biochemistry, and sterol synthesis. In aggregate, the data suggest a primary disruption of galactose utilisation with secondary effects on osmolyte balance (galactitol, myo-inositol) and lipid remodeling. Clinical correlation with hepatic or lens pathology would be warranted given the known toxicity of galactitol accumulation.",
